Yggdrasil on openSUSE
=====================

First start
-----------

  sudo systemctl start yggdrasil

On first start, yggdrasil-default-config.service writes /etc/yggdrasil.conf.
Add peers to it (public list: https://github.com/yggdrasil-network/public-peers),
then:

  sudo systemctl enable --now yggdrasil

Members of the "yggdrasil" group can run yggdrasilctl without root:

  sudo usermod -aG yggdrasil <user>

Firewall
--------

Your Yggdrasil address (200::/7) can be reached by every node of the public
network. Unless told otherwise, firewalld puts the TUN interface in the
default zone (usually "public"), which may expose services such as ssh or
cockpit to all of them.

This package ships a firewalld zone "yggdrasil" (target DROP, no services).
It is not bound to any interface until you bind it:

1. Give the TUN interface a fixed name in /etc/yggdrasil.conf:

     IfName: ygg0

2. Bind it to the zone and restart:

     sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --zone=yggdrasil --add-interface=ygg0
     sudo firewall-cmd --reload
     sudo systemctl restart yggdrasil

To offer a service to your peers, e.g. ssh:

     sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --zone=yggdrasil --add-service=ssh
     sudo firewall-cmd --reload

Multicast and VPNs
------------------

The default MulticastInterfaces regex ".*" also matches VPN interfaces such
as tailscale0 or wg0, which report multicast support but do not carry it.
To limit discovery to the LAN, use something like:

  MulticastInterfaces: [{ Regex: "^(en|eth|wl)", Beacon: true, Listen: true, Port: 0 }]

Yggdrasil and Tailscale can run side by side: they use separate interfaces
and address ranges (200::/7 vs. 100.64.0.0/10 and fd7a:115c:a1e0::/48).
